---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import InjectionChecklist from '../../components/InjectionChecklist.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Prompt Injection Test Checklist" description="An interactive checklist for reviewing your AI application's resistance to prompt injection before shipping.">
  <InjectionChecklist client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>Why this matters</h2>
  <p>Any AI application that processes untrusted input — user messages, web content, uploaded documents — is a potential prompt injection target. This checklist covers the most common gaps, though it's a starting point, not a complete security audit.</p>

  <h2>Common Prompt Injection Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Trusting content fetched from external sources</strong> (web pages, documents) as if it came directly from the user, when it could contain injected instructions.</li>
    <li><strong>Giving the model unrestricted tool access</strong> without guardrails on destructive actions, making a successful injection much more damaging.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Injection Checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Reviewing an AI application's exposure to prompt injection before launch</li>
    <li>Auditing an agent that processes external content (web, documents, emails)</li>
    <li>Establishing security review standards for a team building AI features</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Prompt Injection FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Can prompt injection be fully prevented?</strong> Not with complete certainty — it's an ongoing security challenge similar to other injection classes; the goal is layered mitigations (input handling, tool guardrails, output validation) that reduce risk, not a single fix that eliminates it.</p>
  <p><strong>Does this apply only to chatbots?</strong> No — any system that feeds untrusted content (web pages, documents, emails, user messages) into a model's context is exposed, including agents, RAG pipelines, and automated content processing.</p>
</ToolLayout>
